So, how do you come across? This can sometimes be restricted by persona and perspective, but I like to believe you can make anything work if you try hard enough. Let's start on easy mode...here is a really high level list of tones that I feel truly set the same script, narrative, angle, and even creator/avatar apart. This could make or break performance, depending on whether the tone of your video is communicating the content in a way your audience will be receptive to. By the way, background music and scripting could also help set the tone. If you're tight on resources, re-cut existing content and switch up the sound. it might help more than you think.

Tuning in on tones: 
* **Hyped bestie:** this delivery makes the viewer feel like the creator/person doing the voiceover is super excited to be here with them for 60-90s. It tends to be excited, and is best communicated with an on-screen avatar. Body language and facial expressions both matter to effectively deliver this vibe. 
* **Calm educator:** This person is not a know it all. They're probably a nerd, of their chosen kryptonite. Which could be something as fun as reading romance or as practical as purchasing products for a store. The vibe is laid back and informative without lecturing or boring viewers. They should come across as competent and should communicate high interest in the subject without broaching any of the following: over-enthusiastic, raving obsessive, mindless yapper.
* **Casual Hopecore:** So this has a bit less to do with a specific demeanor, and more to do with the emotion they invoke without overly emoting. The topic may be challenging or "negative" but the content should be communicating resolutions, and the overall effect of the primary speaker in the video should be an optimistic realist who is helping viewers figure out their role in bringing about a change. This tone can be especially powerful when you are communicating a potentially controversial topic to a mixed crowd. You won't give skeptics too much to play with by way of emotion, you'll guide already informed viewers towards their next step, and you'll bring in neutral prospects who will most likely read this from an informational lens.
* **Breezy Brand:** So, you want to produce branded content that isn't so...stiff. You want to lean into organic trends witohut losing alignment. What do you do? Keep that slighlty suited up tone that comes across as confident and educational, but play with pacing and language that is less formal. Don't rush, but don't take a breath. This could be delivered by simultaneously showing viewers key aspects of the brand (a sort of listicle, not so clearly spelled) and just adding v.o. for each item/aspect you introduce. String the watcher into the body of the video wiith a longer/shape-shifting list that leads into the heart of the brand storytelling. Here's an example: "If you're interested in {[topic/adjective] or [topic/adjective] or [topic/adjective]} / {[topic/adjective] like this, this, or this} then {you'll love [brand/service]} / [brand/service] [relevance to persona]." Lead from that montage into a script that speaks about the brand. Again, you don't have to follow this listicle hook format, but it's an example to help you see how you can capture attention with a common format while staying true to brand voice. Delivered faster than an austere tone, but slower than the frantic organic trends are commonly delivered.
* **Uncertain Skeptic:** This person is not snarky or overly sarcastic. However,t hey infuse doubt. In the ideal outcome, your creator comes around to the product/brand/service by the end of the video. This directly captures the attention of anyone who isn't yet sold, challenges their beliefs, and inches them closer to consideration. In a perfect world, you could even convert a hardcore disbeliever, particularly if their experience was outdated, influenced by a different persona, or entirely imagined without actual experience.
* **Playfully Sarcastic:** I'd be very careful with this one. Perhaps I'm just sensitive to sarcasm, but I feel that with your money on the line this could rack up engagement and go sideway with conversions real quick. This is the type of tone that has almost no limits, beyond your personal/brand guidelines. You could use it to directly speak against a competitor claim or customer shade. You could playfully address a real mistake your team made. Whichever way you shape it, make sure there truly is a positive spin and that you aren't stirring up legal trouble or straight up lying, because the comments will come for you.
* **Blunt Brand Voice:** We covered how to transform a tone from buttoned up to breezy, but what about the other way? To me, being blunt in ads neighbors being sarcastic or snarky. Blunt tones can rub people the wrong way, even when there's really nothing wrong with them. Alternatively, they could read as boring. But if you have a brand that's very technical, or needs to convey important information, being blunt could be a good alternative to being austere. You can shift to a more neutral tone, and possibly infuse humor, without laughing or making a joke of yourself. The way I am thinking about it, GI Joe dad speaks to his toddler about why it's important to prioritize self care. Maybe he has a straightforward affect, but you know he cares and you can tell in his delivery. With his child, he won't be harsh, but he will effectively communicate what he's talking about and why it needs to be taken seriously. This is a topic some might avoid broaching head on with their kids, but most people would agree it's important for children to understand this and develop habits with this in mind. Think of your audience, the thing they need to know but maybe don't want to hear, and the way you can be intentional about translating that to them to drive them towards the action you want them to take. Maintain careful modulation, but really lean into scripting to ensure you hit the points just right.
* **Chaotically Urgent:** In my mind, urgency and chaos share the same page. Executively them differently takes intention, and for most paid ads you don't really need one without the other. The news needs to convey urgency sans chaos. However, your Black Friday sale is submerged in a sea of other discounts, and what will break through the noise better than a dose of disorder. Ensure you are communicating clear takeaways, but let the tone take on that chaotic pacing. Let your creator infuse facial expressions that help push this vibe along. Humor can help soften it too. To me, I feel like the yapper is a great perspective from which to test a chaotically urgent tone.
